/-!
# The descent of the difference of two lifts to a map out of the differentials

Let `a : X ⟶ Y` and `b : Y ⟶ T` be morphisms of algebraic spaces over a scheme `S` and let `C`
be a sheaf of `𝒪_X`-modules.  [Stacks Project, Tag 0618] produces, out of two lifts of one
morphism across a square-zero thickening, a `T`-derivation of `𝒪_Y` with values in `a_*C`, and
Equation [Stacks Project, Tag 0619] turns that derivation into an `𝒪_X`-linear map

`θ : a^*Ω_{Y/T} ⟶ C`.

The difference of the two lifts is, to begin with, a derivation not of `𝒪_Y` but of the
structure sheaf `A` of the *thickening* of `Y`, which surjects onto `𝒪_Y` only locally: the map
`A ⟶ 𝒪_Y` of a thickening is an epimorphism of sheaves and is not surjective on the sections
over an object of the site, the obstruction being `H¹` of the defining ideal there.  So the
passage from the difference to a derivation of `𝒪_Y` — the **descent** — is not sectionwise.
This file proves it, and composes it with Equation (0619) to produce `θ`.

The descent is proved in the abelian category of sheaves of abelian groups rather than by a
gluing argument: the quotient map is an epimorphism there, the derivation kills its kernel, and
an epimorphism in an abelian category is the cokernel of its kernel.  The only place where the
sheaf condition is used by hand is the Leibniz rule for the descended family, which is an
equation between two sections that agree on the covering sieve where the two arguments lift.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Sites.descendAlongEpi`: the factorization of a morphism of sheaves of abelian groups
  through an epimorphism it kills the kernel of.
* `Stacks.Sites.QuotientDerivation`: sectionwise derivation data on a sheaf of rings `A`
  *through* a morphism `q : A ⟶ O`, with values in an `O`-module — additive, Leibniz for the
  `O`-action through `q`, compatible with restriction, and vanishing on the sections killed by
  `q`.
* `Stacks.Sites.QuotientDerivation.descend`: the descended derivation of `O`.
* `Stacks.Sites.SquareZeroValues` and `Stacks.Sites.LiftDifferenceData`: the square-zero ideal
  in which the difference of two lifts takes its values, and a pair of lifts into it.  The two
  are separate so that three pairs along a chain of three lifts share one ideal.
* `Stacks.Sites.LiftDifferenceData.toQuotientDerivation`: the difference of two lifts is
  derivation data through `q`, hence descends.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.descendedDerivation`: the descended family as a bundled
  `PresheafOfModules.Derivation'`, that is as a `T`-derivation of `𝒪_Y`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.descendedDifferentialsHom` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.liftDifferenceDifferentialsHom`: **the map `θ : a^*Ω_{Y/T} ⟶ C`**,
  from a descended derivation and from a pair of lifts respectively.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Sites.existsUnique_descendAlongEpi`: the descent is the unique factorization.
* `Stacks.Sites.QuotientDerivation.descend_app`: the descended derivation computes the given
  one, `D̄(q x) = D x`.
* `Stacks.Sites.QuotientDerivation.descend_mul`: the Leibniz rule for the descended derivation.
  This is the one step which is local: both sides are compared on the covering sieve where the
  two sections lift.
* `Stacks.Sites.QuotientDerivation.descend_add` and
  `Stacks.Sites.QuotientDerivation.descend_eq_zero_of_d_eq_zero`: the descent is additive in
  the derivation and kills the zero derivation.  These are what make the differences along a
  chain of lifts add up.
* `Stacks.Sites.app_eq_of_app_eq_zero_of_mem_span`: **hypothesis (2) of
  [Stacks Project, Tag 0618] from a generating family.**  Two lifts into a square-zero ideal
  agree on the sections killed by `q` as soon as the kernel of `q` is, over a covering sieve,
  generated by sections killed by `q` on which they agree: their difference is a derivation for
  the action through the first lift, so it is determined on an ideal by its values on
  generators.
* `Stacks.Sites.LiftDifferenceData.diff_mul`: the Leibniz rule for the difference of two lifts,
  where the square-zero hypothesis is spent, and
  `Stacks.Sites.LiftDifferenceData.diff_add_diff`: the differences along a chain of three lifts
  add up.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.descendedDifferentialsHom_spec` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.liftDifferenceDifferentialsHom_spec`: the characterizing equation of
  `θ`, that its transpose along `a^* ⊣ a_*` carries the universal differential `d_{Y/T}(t)` to
  the value of the descended derivation at `t`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.existsUnique_descendedDifferentialsHom` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.eq_descendedDifferentialsHom_of_spec`: `θ` is the unique morphism
  with that property, Equation (0619) being a bijection.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.liftDifferenceDifferentialsHom_add` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.liftDifferenceDifferentialsHom_eq_zero`: the linear maps add along a
  chain of three lifts, and a lift does not differ from itself.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.additive_modulePushforward` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.additive_modulePullback`: the two module functors of Stacks 03LU are
  additive, which is what makes the transposition of Equation (0619) additive and hence the
  differences add.

## Implementation notes

**Why the descent is an abelian-category factorization and not a gluing argument.**  A
derivation `D : A ⟶ F` killing `ker q` factors through `q` as soon as `q` is an epimorphism and
`ker q` is a kernel of it, which in an abelian category is automatic
(`CategoryTheory.Abelian.epiDesc`).  Sheaves of abelian groups on the small étale site of an
algebraic space form an abelian category — sheafification exists there, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.SmallEtale.instHasSheafify` — and a locally surjective morphism of
sheaves of rings is an epimorphism of the underlying sheaves of abelian groups, since the two
morphisms have the same components and therefore the same image sieves.  So no covering family
has to be chosen and no section has to be glued.  The alternative, defining the descended
derivation on a cover and gluing, needs the surjectivity of `A(V) ⟶ 𝒪_Y(V)` on affine objects
and a compatibility check on overlaps; it is not used.

**Where the sheaf condition is still used.**  In the Leibniz rule
`Stacks.Sites.QuotientDerivation.descend_mul`.  The descended family is only known to compute
the given derivation on the sections in the image of `q`, so for two arbitrary sections `x, y`
of `𝒪_Y` both sides of the rule are compared after restriction to the intersection of the two
image sieves, where `x` and `y` lift; the target being a sheaf, hence separated, the two
sections are then equal.  `CategoryTheory.Presheaf.localPreimage` provides the lifts.

**Why the values of the difference are given as a square-zero ideal with an injection.**  The
difference of the two lifts is a section of the ambient sheaf of rings `𝒜` and the module `C`
of values has to be identified with the ideal it lands in.  That identification is the datum
`Stacks.Sites.SquareZeroValues`: an additive injection `ι : M ⟶ 𝒜` compatible with restriction
whose image has square zero, together with the commutativity of the sections of `𝒜`.  The
commutativity is a field because the ambient object is a `Sheaf J RingCat` — the structure
sheaves of this development are indexed over `RingCat`, not `CommRingCat`, which is why the
same hypothesis appears as `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ringSheaf_mul_comm_smallEtaleRingedSite`
elsewhere in the library.  It is used in exactly one place, the Leibniz rule
`Stacks.Sites.LiftDifferenceData.diff_mul`, through the ring identity
`a * b - c * d = a * (b - d) + b * (a - c)` which holds when `(a - c) * (b - d) = 0`.

**Why the `𝒪`-action on the module is a field and not a conclusion.**  The ideal of values is
an `𝒜`-module, and the action factors through the reduction of `𝒜` because the ideal has square
zero; what the descent needs is the action of `𝒪_Y`, and that is the action through `f`.  The
field `ι_smul` records exactly this, on the sections in the image of `q`, which is what the
Leibniz rule consumes.  Producing the `𝒪_Y`-action from the `𝒜`-action would itself be a
descent, of a bilinear map, and is not needed: in the geometric situation the module is
`a_*C` and is an `𝒪_Y`-module to begin with.

**What is taken as given and what is proved.**  The two lifts are an arbitrary pair of
morphisms of sheaves of rings into one ambient sheaf of rings on the small étale site of `Y`.
Identifying the two comorphisms of two morphisms of thickenings as a pair of maps with one and
the same target is the structure-sheaf half of [Stacks Project, Tag 05ZM]; that identification
is available unconditionally in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/Thickenings/LiftDifferenceCommonTarget.lean` and in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/Thickenings/BaseChangeOfEqualRestriction.lean`, and the
square-zero ideal of values at an arbitrary target space is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.squareZeroValuesAt`.  What is proved here is the descent,
unconditionally and for an arbitrary pair of maps.  No `@[stacks 0618]` attribute is placed by
this file because the remaining step of that tag's content — matching hypothesis (2) of the
source, the equality of the two maps of conormal modules of
[Stacks Project, Tag 04CP], against the agreement of the two maps on the defining ideal which
the data below take as a field — is stated by no declaration; the "Partial coverage" section of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/LiftDifferenceDerivation.lean` records that.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0618](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0618)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0619](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0619), the transposition of a
  derivation into a map out of the differentials.
* [Stacks Project, Tag 04CT](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04CT), the sheaf of
